==== NAME
pdxdig -- DNS query CLI (`dig`-equivalent) for paideia-os.

==== SYNOPSIS
    pdxdig <name>
    pdxdig --dry-run <name>
    pdxdig --type=A <name>
    pdxdig --type=<other> <name>
    pdxdig --server=IP.IP.IP.IP <name>
    pdxdig --timeout-ms=<n> <name>

Flag/positional order is unconstrained; the last positional argv slot
wins (matches `dig(1)`'s own tail-argument convention). Long flags may
appear in any position and in any combination.

==== DESCRIPTION
`pdxdig` is a standalone ring-3 ELF -- its own `_start`, no shared
library, no dynamic linker -- that issues an `A`-record DNS query and
reports the result. It is one of seven R100 user-space networking
tools; see `design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md` §6 + §13.5 in
the paideia-os monorepo for the wave charter this tool's issues are
filed against. Its wave siblings are [[cref: pdxsock#NAME]],
[[cref: pdxping#NAME]] and [[cref: pdxtrust#NAME]].

[[posix: there is no `/etc/resolv.conf` and no stub resolver library
underneath this tool. `--server=` is the only way to name a resolver;
absent it, the tool has no notion of "the system nameserver" -- resolver
selection is argv-level, not a hidden environment lookup.]]

At v1.4.0 the real UDP query round-trip is BLOCKED on the kernel-side
`SOCK_DGRAM` extension (R100-PREP-002, design doc §12.1): every
invocation marshals and emits a `DnsQueryRecord@0.1` on the semantic
pipe, then a `libpdx-audit` fingerprint (STUB body today), and the
no-flag path exits 3 with `pdxdig: udp stub` on fd 2 rather than
performing a live query. See [[cref: LIMITATIONS]].

==== OPTIONS

---- --dry-run
Skip the (currently stubbed) query round-trip and take the REAL
`DRY_RUN` exit path deterministically: emits a `DnsQueryRecord@0.1`
with `result_code = DRY_RUN` (5), writes
`pdxdig: dry-run ok` to fd 2, exits 0.

---- --type=<QTYPE>
Only `A` is supported at v1.4.0 (`libpdx-net`'s resolver parses no
other RR type). `--type=A` is accepted as equivalent to omitting the
flag. Any other value is refused cleanly with `UNSUPPORTED_QTYPE`
(exit 4) rather than attempted and misparsed.

---- --server=IP.IP.IP.IP
Parses a dotted-quad IPv4 address (0..255 per octet, exactly three
`.` separators) via `ArgvParse::argv_parse_ipv4` and stores it as the
resolver override. A malformed value (empty octet, octet > 255, wrong
octet count, non-digit byte) is folded into the same usage-refusal
bucket (exit 2) an unknown `-flag` uses -- it is not a distinct error
class from the caller's point of view. The parsed value is not yet
consumed by any resolver-selection code (see
[[cref: LIMITATIONS]]); it is stored for the M2-001 real query path
to read once that lands.

---- --timeout-ms=<n>
Recognized and accepted; presently a no-op. No elevate needed for any
flag here -- UDP DNS queries take no more privilege than the TCP
sockets other R100 tools already use.

==== EXIT-CODES

---- 0
`--dry-run` path taken. Record emitted with `result_code = DRY_RUN`.

---- 2
Argv usage refusal: no positional argument given, an unrecognized
`-flag` seen, or a malformed `--server=` value. Emits `pdxdig: usage`
on fd 2.

---- 3
No-flag path taken at v1.4.0: the real UDP query is not yet wired
(BLOCKED on R100-PREP-002). Emits `pdxdig: udp stub` on fd 2 after
still marshalling and emitting the semantic-pipe record.

---- 4
`--type=<non-A>` refusal (`UNSUPPORTED_QTYPE`). Takes precedence over
`--dry-run` if both conditions are present in one invocation -- a
rejected `--type=` is a client-side error regardless of dry-run
intent.

==== RECORD
Each invocation emits a 48-byte `DnsQueryRecord@0.1` on the
semantic-pipe ring via `sys_semantic_send` (SC+ ID 115), schema tag
`0x446E735172797201`:

    [ +0]  magic         [u8; 8]     "PDXKDIG_" ASCII
    [ +8]  version:u32 | flags:u32
    [+16]  audit_id      u64         zero at v1.4.0
    [+24]  qname_hash    u64         rolling-add placeholder
    [+32]  qtype:u16 | answer_ip:u32 | ttl_hi:u16   -- qtype=1 (A) always
    [+40]  ttl_lo:u16 | cname_hops:u8 | txn_id:u16 | result_code:u8 | reserved:u16

`result_code` enum: `OK`=0, `NXDOMAIN`=1, `TIMEOUT`=2,
`MALFORMED_RESPONSE`=3, `UNSUPPORTED_QTYPE`=4, `DRY_RUN`=5. Only 4 and
5 are reachable at v1.4.0; 0..3 are wired by the shared response
walker (`src/dns_walker.pdx`) and exercised today only against the
tests' mocked fixtures, not a live socket.

==== AUDIT
Once per argv-accepted invocation, `AuditWire::pdxdig_audit_log_query`
is called immediately after the `sys_semantic_send` emission. It is a
STUB at v1.4.0 -- `libpdx-audit` is not yet in this repo's `deps:` --
and always returns `AFS_OK` (0); the real body will call
`AuditFileSink::audit_file_append("/system/audit/pdxdig.log", ...)`
with a compact query-name/qtype/rcode fingerprint line once that
dependency lands (M3-001-b follow-up).

==== LIMITATIONS
- The real UDP query round-trip (M2-001) is BLOCKED on
  R100-PREP-002 (§12.1 kernel-side `SOCK_DGRAM` extension).
  `src/dig_query.pdx` documents the real `net_resolve` extern
  contract and ships a WEAK-STUB `dig_query_resolve` returning the
  `0xDEADBEEF` sentinel; it is not yet called from `_start`.
- `--server=` is parsed and stored but not yet consumed by any
  resolver-selection code -- no real resolver path exists yet to read
  it (M2-001-b follow-up).
- `--timeout-ms=` is recognized but semantics are a no-op (M2-004
  follow-up).
- The `libpdx-audit` fingerprint call is a STUB (see
  [[cref: AUDIT]]); wiring the real dependency is gated on it landing
  without breaking this repo's flat single-ELF `--fatal-warnings`
  link.
- `manifest.pdxsig`'s dual signature blocks (Ed25519 + ML-DSA-65) are
  documented placeholders pending live release-line key material --
  see `release/RELEASE-1.4.0.md` §3.

